# Voyagely – Git Workflow & Conventions

## Branch Strategy

### Protected Branch

- `main` is the **stable branch**. It must always build, pass tests, and be deployable.
- **Never commit directly to `main`.** Always work on a feature/fix branch and merge via PR (or controlled merge).

### Branch Naming

```
feature/issue-XX-short-description
fix/issue-XX-short-description
docs/short-description
refactor/short-description
chore/short-description
```

Examples:

- `feature/issue-1-database-schema`
- `fix/issue-5-voting-duplicate`
- `docs/update-roadmap-phase2`
- `refactor/trip-detail-split-components`

### Branch Lifecycle

1. Create branch from `main` (or current development branch).
2. Work on the branch. Commit often with clear messages.
3. Push to remote. Open PR when ready.
4. Merge into `main` after review / tests pass.
5. Delete the branch after merge.

## Commit Messages – Conventional Commits

Use the [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) format:

```
<type>: <short description>

[optional body with more details]
```

### Types

| Type       | When to use                                      |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `feat`     | New feature or significant new functionality     |
| `fix`      | Bug fix                                          |
| `docs`     | Documentation only (markdown, comments)          |
| `refactor` | Code restructuring without behavior change       |
| `test`     | Adding or updating tests                         |
| `chore`    | Maintenance (deps, config, CI, tooling)          |
| `style`    | Code style changes (formatting, no logic change) |
| `perf`     | Performance improvement                          |

### Examples

```
feat: add constraint engine for trip budget and pace

fix: prevent duplicate votes on same activity

docs: update roadmap to move expenses to Phase 2

refactor: split TripDetailPage into feature sub-components

test: add E2E spec for activity creation flow

chore: update Supabase types after migration 007
```

### Rules

- Subject line: **imperative mood** ("add", "fix", "update"), not past tense ("added", "fixed").
- Subject line: **< 72 characters**.
- Body (optional): explain **why**, not just what. The diff shows the what.
- Reference issue numbers when relevant: `feat: add voting system (Issue #10)`.

## Pull Request Guidelines

### PR Size

- Aim for **< 400 lines of diff** per PR.
- If a feature requires more, break it into sequential PRs:
  - PR 1: Data model + migrations.
  - PR 2: Hooks + services.
  - PR 3: UI components.
  - PR 4: Tests.

### PR Description

Every PR should include:

```markdown
## Summary

- What this PR does (1-3 bullets).

## Related Issue

- Issue #XX

## Test Plan

- [ ] Unit tests added/updated
- [ ] E2E tests added/updated (if user-facing)
- [ ] Manual testing done (describe steps)

## Screenshots (if UI change)
```

### Before Merging

A PR is ready to merge when:

- [ ] TypeScript compiles with no errors (`pnpm typecheck`).
- [ ] All tests pass (`pnpm test:run`).
- [ ] No new linter warnings.
- [ ] The feature works as described (manual verification).
- [ ] No secrets or `.env` values committed.

## AI Agent – Git Rules

When an AI agent (Cursor, Copilot, etc.) works on the codebase:

- **Commits**: The agent can create local commits freely.
  - **Commit often during large changes**: When modifying many or large files over time, commit at logical steps (e.g. after a feature slice or migration). Avoid one huge commit with 1000+ lines — it makes regressions hard to track and bisect.
  - **Skip commits for trivial edits**: Do not commit for a single-line or very small change; use judgment.
- **Push**: The agent must **never push** to remote unless the user explicitly says "push" or "ok push".
- **Force push**: **Never**. Not even if the user asks (warn first).
- **Amend**: Only if the commit was just created in the current session and hasn't been pushed.
- **Branch switching**: Ask before switching branches if there are uncommitted changes.

